# Summary
Introduced a denial-of-service vector by removing per-PG query caps for delimiter listings, causing unbounded DB reads and allocations.
The commit changes ListObjectsV2 to use u32::MAX as the per‑PG limit whenever a delimiter is supplied. The coordinator then requests that many rows from each placement group and collects them all into a Vec before applying delimiter logic and max-keys truncation. In the storage layer, list_objects uses req.max_keys to set the SQL LIMIT and eagerly pushes every row into a vector. With the new per‑PG limit, a single authenticated request with a delimiter (even with default max-keys) can force the server to enumerate the entire bucket across PGs, consuming excessive CPU/memory and risking OOM or prolonged stalls. This is a network-reachable availability issue for any user with valid credentials (or for public buckets if listing is exposed).

# Resolution
Fixed in commit `daa1b76` (`Fix bounded delimiter object listing`).

The shipped fix removes the unbounded delimiter fetch path in `ListObjectsV2`.
Delimiter listings now page per placement group in bounded batches and use
lexicographic jumps over common-prefix subtrees instead of requesting
effectively unbounded result sets. The storage layer was extended with
inclusive `start_at` support so the coordinator can resume from computed upper
bounds without scanning entire prefixes.

