# Attack-path analysis
Final: medium | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: medium | Policy adjusted: medium
## Rationale
Impact is limited to availability and requires authenticated access, but a single delimiter-based list request can cause unbounded DB reads and memory allocation (coordinator.rs:438-460; pg_store.rs:392-534). Given in-scope authenticated clients and network exposure, medium severity is appropriate.
## Likelihood
high - Triggerable by any authenticated client with a delimiter parameter; requires access credentials and a sufficiently large bucket to exhaust resources. | Remote network vector
## Impact
medium - Unbounded listing can trigger large SQL scans and memory allocations across PGs, risking OOM or prolonged service stalls (availability impact).
## Assumptions
- The service is deployed with ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR reachable by the attacker (not strictly localhost-only).
- The attacker has valid S3 credentials for the Argmin server.
- Target buckets can contain enough objects for unbounded listing to stress memory/CPU.
- Valid S3 access key/secret
- ListObjectsV2 request with a delimiter parameter
- Bucket with large object count
## Path
client -> http -> coordinator -> pg_store -> impact
## Path evidence
- `crates/server/src/http/mod.rs:69-84` - ListObjectsV2 reads attacker-controlled delimiter/max-keys and calls coordinator.list_objects_v2.
- `crates/server/src/http/mod.rs:119-135` - All requests require SigV4 authentication, establishing the credential precondition.
- `crates/server/src/coordinator.rs:438-460` - per_pg_limit is set to u32::MAX when delimiter is provided, and all PG results are collected.
- `crates/storage/src/pg_store.rs:392-534` - SQL LIMIT derives from req.max_keys and rows are eagerly pushed into a Vec before truncation.
- `crates/server/src/config.rs:19-34` - Server listens on configurable address (default 127.0.0.1:9000) and uses env-provided credentials.
## Narrative
ListObjectsV2 takes attacker-controlled query params (delimiter/max-keys) after authentication, and passes them into list_objects_v2 (server/src/http/mod.rs:69-84). When delimiter is present, Coordinator sets per_pg_limit to u32::MAX and aggregates all PG results into memory (server/src/coordinator.rs:438-460). The storage layer uses req.max_keys to build SQL LIMIT and pushes every row into a Vec before truncation (storage/src/pg_store.rs:392-534), so u32::MAX produces effectively unbounded DB reads and allocations. An authenticated request with delimiter can therefore exhaust CPU/memory and stall or crash the service. No runtime PoC was executed due to build constraints; analysis is static.
## Controls
- SigV4 authentication required for all S3 operations
- Configurable listen address (default 127.0.0.1:9000)
- No explicit rate limiting on list operations
## Blindspots
- No runtime PoC due to build/dependency download constraints.
- Deployment exposure (public vs localhost-only) depends on ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR configuration.
